  }}
  * {{ box-sizing: border-box; }}
  body {{
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }}
  .container {{
    max-width: 1100px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px 24px;
  }}
  header {{
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--primary);
    padding-bottom: 18px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }}
  h1 {{
    font-size: 1.9rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    margin: 0 0 6px 0;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }}
  .subtitle {{
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0;
  }}
  .meta {{
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 8px;
  }}
  .kpi-grid {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 24px 0 32px 0;
  }}
  .kpi-card {{
    background: var(--card);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 20px 18px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
    border-left: 5px solid var(--primary);
  }}
  .kpi-card.accent {{ border-left-color: var(--accent); }}
  .kpi-value {{
    font-size: 1.8rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--primary);
    margin: 0;
  }}
  .kpi-value.accent {{ color: var(--accent); }}
  .kpi-label {{
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.4px;
    margin-top: 4px;
  }}
  section {{
    background: var(--card);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 28px;
    margin-bottom: 24px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
  }}
  h2 {{
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    margin: 0 0 14px 0;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--primary);
    padding-left: 12px;
  }}
  h3 {{
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 20px 0 10px 0;
  }}
  p {{
    margin: 0 0 12px 0;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }}
  .chart {{
    margin: 18px 0;
    text-align: center;
  }}
  .chart img {{
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }}
  table {{
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 14px 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }}
  th, td {{
    padding: 10px 12px;
    text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }}
  th {{
    background: #f0f3f7;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }}
  tr:hover {{ background: #fafbfc; }}
  .note {{
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    background: #f7f9fb;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--primary);
    margin: 14px 0;
  }}
  .highlight {{
    background: #fff8e1;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }}
  ul {{
    margin: 8px 0;
    padding-left: 22px;
  }}
  li {{ margin-bottom: 6px; }}
  .two-col {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(320px, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
    align-items: start;
  }}
  footer {{
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 10px;
    padding-bottom: 30px;
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
  <header>
    <h1>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit — 2026 Catalog Analysis</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A data-driven assessment of the 14,875 operational spacecraft currently on orbit.</p>
    <p class="meta">Generated: {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')} &nbsp;|&nbsp; Source: CelesTrak / NORAD TLE Catalog</p>
  </header>

  <!-- KPIs -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{summary['total_satellites']:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Active Satellites</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{summary['leo_share']:.1f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">LEO Share</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent">
      <div class="kpi-value accent">{summary['starlink_count']:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Starlink Constellation</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{summary['median_inclination']:.1f}°</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Median Inclination</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent">
      <div class="kpi-value accent">{summary['since_2020']:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Launched Since 2020</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Overview -->
  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Catalog Overview & Key Figures</h2>
    <p>
      The CelesTrak active-satellite catalog contains <strong>{summary['total_satellites']:,}</strong> operational spacecraft as of early 2026.
      The dataset spans launch years from <strong>{min(launch_counts.keys())}</strong> to <strong>{max(launch_counts.keys())}</strong>,
      though the overwhelming majority of currently active assets were launched within the last six years.
    </p>
    <p>
      Orbital parameters show a strong concentration in near-circular, low-inclination orbits typical of commercial mega-constellations.
      Median inclination sits at <strong>{summary['median_inclination']:.1f}°</strong>, driven by the large Starlink population at ~53°.
      Median eccentricity is very low (≈0.0001), confirming that most operational satellites follow nearly circular orbits.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Data freshness:</strong> Epoch dates in the catalog are concentrated in March 2026, indicating the TLE sets are current.
      All statistics below are computed directly from the raw catalog; no extrapolation or modeling is applied.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Orbital Regime -->
  <section id="orbit-regime">
    <h2>Orbital Regime Classification</h2>
    <p>
      Satellites were classified using <strong>mean motion (revolutions per day)</strong> thresholds derived from standard orbital mechanics:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>LEO</strong> — Mean motion &gt; 14 rev/day (roughly 160–2,000 km altitude)</li>
      <li><strong>MEO</strong> — Mean motion between 1.0 and 14 rev/day</li>
      <li><strong>GEO</strong> — Mean motion ≈ 0.9–1.0 rev/day (geosynchronous altitude ~35,786 km)</li>
      <li><strong>HEO</strong> — Eccentricity &gt; 0.5 (highly elliptical orbits, e.g., Molniya)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['orbit_regime']}" alt="Orbital Regime Distribution">
    </div>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Regime</th><th>Count</th><th>Share</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>LEO</td><td>{leo_count:,}</td><td>{leo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MEO</td><td>{meo_count:,}</td><td>{meo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GEO</td><td>{geo_count:,}</td><td>{geo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HEO</td><td>{heo_count:,}</td><td>{heo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      LEO dominates the catalog, hosting <strong>{leo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%</strong> of all active satellites.
      This reflects the rapid deployment of broadband mega-constellations. GEO, once the premier commercial orbit,
      now represents only <strong>{geo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%</strong> of the active fleet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Constellation Analysis -->
  <section id="constellation-analysis">
    <h2>Major Constellation & Operator Breakdown</h2>
    <p>
      Parsing <code>OBJECT_NAME</code> prefixes reveals that a single operator — SpaceX's Starlink — accounts for
      <strong>{starlink_pct:.1f}%</strong> of all active satellites ({summary['starlink_count']:,} spacecraft).
      Removing Starlink leaves {non_starlink:,} satellites, a figure still larger than the entire global fleet as recently as 2019.
    </p>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['constellations']}" alt="Top Constellations">
    </div>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Operator / Constellation</th><th>Active Satellites</th><th>Share of Total</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
'''

for name, count in top_const:
    pct = count / summary['total_satellites'] * 100
    html += f'        <tr><td>{name}</td><td>{count:,}</td><td>{pct:.1f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''        <tr><td>Other</td><td>{summary['constellation_counts']['Other']:,}</td><td>{summary['constellation_counts']['Other']/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Implication for analysts:</strong> Any aggregate statistic that treats all satellites equally is effectively a Starlink statistic.
      Policy assessments of spectrum use, conjunction risk, or debris generation should segment by operator or constellation to avoid misleading averages.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Launch Trends -->
  <section id="launch-trends">
    <h2>Launch Year Trends</h2>
    <p>
      The growth curve of active satellites is the headline story of this catalog.
      Before 2020, the active fleet grew gradually; since 2020 it has exploded.
      Of the {summary['total_satellites']:,} active satellites, <strong>{summary['since_2020']:,}</strong> ({summary['since_2020']/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%)
      were launched in 2020 or later, while only {pre_2020:,} pre-date that threshold.
    </p>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['launch_trends']}" alt="Active Satellite Population by Launch Year">
    </div>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['launch_recent']}" alt="Launch Activity 2015-2026">
    </div>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['cumulative']}" alt="Cumulative Active Satellite Population">
    </div>
    <p>
      The peak single-year contribution comes from <strong>{peak_year}</strong>, with {peak_val:,} currently active satellites.
      The post-2020 surge is almost entirely attributable to Starlink batch launches and, more recently, Amazon's Kuiper initial deployment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Orbital Characteristics -->
  <section id="orbital-characteristics">
    <h2>Orbital Parameter Deep Dive</h2>
    <p>
      Beyond population counts, the catalog's orbital parameters reveal how operators are using physical space.
    </p>

    <h3>Inclination</h3>
    <p>
      Inclination distribution is bimodal: a massive peak near <strong>53°</strong> (Starlink shells) and a secondary cluster near
      <strong>97–98°</strong> (sun-synchronous orbits used by imaging and weather satellites).
      The median across all satellites is <strong>{summary['median_inclination']:.1f}°</strong>.
    </p>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['inclination']}" alt="Inclination Distribution">
    </div>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['inclination_by_regime']}" alt="Inclination by Regime">
    </div>

    <h3>Eccentricity</h3>
    <p>
      Nearly all active satellites have eccentricities below 0.1, confirming operational preference for circular orbits
      that simplify station-keeping and payload pointing. A small tail above 0.5 represents HEO assets (e.g., Molniya orbits).
    </p>
    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['eccentricity']}" alt="Eccentricity Distribution">
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Conclusions -->
  <section id="conclusions">
    <h2>Key Takeaways & Outlook</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>LEO is the new normal.</strong> With {leo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}% of active satellites in LEO,
        spectrum allocation and debris-mitigation policy must prioritize low-altitude regimes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Starlink reshapes every aggregate.</strong> At {summary['starlink_count']:,} satellites, SpaceX's constellation is larger than
        the rest of the active fleet combined. Analysts should always present segmented and un-segmented views.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Post-2020 growth is unprecedented.</strong> {summary['since_2020']:,} of {summary['total_satellites']:,} active satellites
        launched in the last six years. Licensing regimes and space-traffic-management systems designed for a slower era are now under strain.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GEO is stable but shrinking in share.</strong> GEO remains vital for broadcast and weather, yet its share of the active fleet
        has fallen to {geo_count/summary['total_satellites']*100:.1f}%. Spectrum re-farming and orbital-slot coordination in GEO still matter,
        but the center of gravity has shifted to LEO.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Orbital shells are highly structured.</strong> The tight clustering of inclination and near-zero eccentricity shows that
        operators are deploying into discrete, pre-planned shells. This predictability aids collision-avoidance planning but also concentrates risk.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Methodology note:</strong> All figures are derived directly from the CelesTrak active-satellite catalog (<code>data.csv</code>).
      Orbital regimes were classified using mean-motion thresholds stated above. Constellation labels were assigned by prefix-matching
      <code>OBJECT_NAME</code>. Launch years were extracted from the first four characters of <code>OBJECT_ID</code>.
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    Report generated by automated analysis pipeline &nbsp;|&nbsp; {datetime.now().strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M UTC')}
  </footer>
</div>
</body>
</html>
'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w') as f:
    f.write(html)

print("Report written to /app/output/final_report.html")
